SUMMARY: The research task is to assess whether end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. The web search provided a query related to the task but did not return any specific findings. The paper search yielded a query related to the task and found several relevant papers. These papers generally support the idea that end-to-end task success provides a more comprehensive measure of model quality, as it accounts for the entire system's performance. However, some papers also highlight that per-call systems metrics can be useful for diagnosing specific issues within the system. The data analysis is not provided, and the fact-check agent did not contribute any findings. In summary, the evidence from the papers suggests that end-to-end task success is a better lever for separating model quality from systems performance, but per-call systems metrics can provide valuable insights for system diagnostics.
